From 3545700c6da4b743015174214ade3d1534de4f83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:49:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVBridge: present a frame ahead where the GPU needs most of a
 refresh period

A frame of native Dolby Vision output was presented the way that waits:
glFinish() for its GPU work, then a blocking atomic commit that returns
with the flip. The next frame could only be drawn from then on, so the
GPU had one refresh period for each frame, less what the rest of the loop
takes. On an integrated GPU the reconstruction of a 2160p picture with
its enhancement layer takes 30 ms composed and 35 to 37 ms drawn directly,
of the 41.7 ms of a film refresh (Raphael): a frame that takes a
little longer misses its refresh, which is seen as a short stutter.

Kodi's ordinary frames do not wait: they go to the kernel with the fence
of their GPU work as IN_FENCE_FD, in a non-blocking commit, and the loop
waits for the flip of the frame before. The Dolby Vision branch, which
comes from CroqueMr's fork, bypassed that. Now a transport frame can be
presented the same way, a frame ahead (CDVAhead):

- EGL native fence of the frame instead of glFinish(), handed to the
  commit; the commit does not wait (FlipPageTransactional() and
  FlipDVBridgePage() take it as a request and tell what was done). The
  fence is made after the buffer swap, so that it also stands for what
  the swap has the GPU do with the buffer (radeonsi: flush_resource),
  which an explicit fence makes the kernel stop waiting for by itself;
- the flip of the frame before is waited for by its out-fence, with a
  timeout, while the GPU already works on this frame. Not eglWaitSyncKHR
  on it, as the ordinary path does: that makes the GPU wait too
  (radeonsi: "all unflushed commands will not start execution before
  this fence dependency is signalled"), which is what this is meant to
  avoid;
- the buffer that was on the screen is kept until the flip that replaces
  it (CGBMSurface: one retired buffer), and given back with the commit
  after that, which is made only once that flip has come;
- the render manager is told one refresh period more of latency through
  GetFrameLatencyAdjustment(), so a frame is chosen one period earlier
  and reaches the screen when it did before.

Fail closed, frame by frame. A commit that is refused with its fences is
made again at once without them, the way that waits, as before this
change, and the frame is not lost. A frame for which the GPU gives no
fence, or whose predecessor's flip is not reported within a second, is
committed the way that waits; so is every frame of an output that has no
third buffer. Three such frames end it for that native output, unless a
minute of frames presented a frame ahead (1440 of them) lies between two
of them; three such outputs in a row, or an output without a third
buffer, until Kodi is started again. A commit that the output refuses
either way is not counted, and a flip that is not reported is counted
once. The first transport frame, every commit with a modeset, every frame
while the output is meant to be off and the restoration of the ordinary
output wait as before. The restoration, and turning the display off or
on, first wait for the flip of the last frame themselves: nvidia-drm
applies a blocking commit at once and waits for the flips before it only
then.

A frame that carries no fence leaves the out-fence that the ordinary path
left where it is: where no frame is presented a frame ahead, the waiting
path is what it was.

It is used where it is needed: when the frames of a playing video take
half a refresh period or more of GPU time on average over a second (all
frames, with GUI elements or without), until they take 35 % or less.
What was found is kept for the following videos at the same refresh
rate. A discrete GPU that draws these frames in 7 to 18 ms keeps the way
that waits at film rates, and takes this one at 50 or 60 Hz. New setting
videoscreen.hadesdvahead (on) turns it off; advancedsettings.xml
video/dvaheadalways asks for it on any GPU, to try it there.

The statistics tell the frames that were presented this way and the times
between the flips of two of them in a row, as the kernel reports them
(the longest, and how many over 1.5 refresh periods): a missed refresh
shows there.

Nothing of this can be run without a display: validated in hardware
only. The decision is covered by a host test (dvahead_test.cpp); the
sequence of fences, commits and buffers is not.
